      <map>
        <entry key="AIAssistant.VCS.GenerateCommitMessage">
          <value>
            <AIAssistantStoredInstruction>
              <option name="actionId" value="AIAssistant.VCS.GenerateCommitMessage" />
              <option name="content" value="Generate a commit message following the Conventional Commits 1.0.0 specification.&#10;&#10;The message should start with a type, followed by a colon and a brief imperative description under 50 characters. Valid types include:&#10;&#10;feat: A new feature&#10;fix: A bug fix&#10;docs: Documentation changes&#10;style: Code styling and formatting changes (not for css/scss styling changes)&#10;refactor: Code refactoring&#10;perf: Performance improvements&#10;test: Adding or modifying tests&#10;build: Build system or dependencies changes&#10;ci: Continuous integration changes&#10;chore: Other non-code changes&#10;After the header, leave a blank line followed by 1-3 sentences with additional details as the optional body.&#10;&#10;Write the body in a neutral tone, avoiding first-person pronouns like &quot;I&quot; or &quot;we&quot;. Focus on concisely summarizing the main changes made without unnecessarily embellishing the purpose or stating obvious benefits.&#10;&#10;Omit any mentions of a scope, breaking changes, or references to issues or pull requests.&#10;Additional guidelines:&#10;- For changes specifically to CSS files that affect the visual aspect of the project, such as updating color variables or adjusting styles for better responsiveness, consider using:&#10;  - `feat`: When the changes significantly alter the user interface or introduce new styles that contribute to new functionality.&#10;  - `chore`: For minor adjustments or optimizations that refine the visual appearance without adding new features.&#10;- The `style` type should be reserved for changes that do not affect the code's logic but improve the formatting, such as adjusting indentation, whitespace, or other non-functional aspects of the code.&#10;&#10;Please ensure that changes to CSS files, particularly those that impact the design or aesthetics, are accurately described to reflect the nature of the change rather than the broader `style` category used for code formatting improvements. Dont write 'this commit/update'. Just write what it does" />
            </AIAssistantStoredInstruction>
          </value>
        </entry>
      </map>
    </option>
